        "date":  "2026-09-16",
        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "Cisco\u0027s Outshift research identifies a \u0027Communication-Reasoning Gap\u0027 in multi-agent AI systems -- agents can exchange messages but fail to convert interaction into shared understanding or coordinated action. The proposed \u0027Internet of Cognition\u0027 architecture addresses this through shared intent protocols, a cognition fabric for governed context and memory, and negotiation engines. Testing with the open-source Mycelium coordination layer improved agent alignment rates from 36% to approximately 93%.",
        "execSummary":  "The strategic implication here is that simply deploying and connecting AI agents is insufficient -- enterprises must now invest in orchestration infrastructure that enables agents to share meaning, not just messages. Cisco\u0027s research quantifies the gap: uncoordinated agents align only 36% of the time, versus 93% with a proper cognition layer. For customer-support and enterprise operations leaders, this means evaluating not just individual agent performance but the quality of inter-agent coordination. The key investment decision shifts from \u0027how many agents\u0027 to \u0027how well do they reason together and stay accountable to human operators.\u0027",
